<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sultan Husayn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1694">1694</a>–<a href="/years/1722">1722</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Shah Sultan Husayn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Husayn, also known as Shah Sultan Husayn, was the Safavid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from 1694 to 1722. His weak rule saw the decline of the state. He surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> to Afghan invaders in 1722, ending effective Safavid rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sultan-husayn.jpg" alt="Sultan Husayn" width="291" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Muhammad Ali (painter)Cropped: TRAJAN 117 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Husayn_of_Persia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Soltan Hoseyn was born in 1668 in the royal harem, the eldest son of Shah Solayman and a Circassian woman, and grew up with little experience of the affairs of the country. He read the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> under Mir Mohammad-Baqer Khatunabadi and preferred Azeri Turkish to Persian. On his deathbed Solayman is reported to have said that the younger son, Soltan Tahmasp, should be chosen for fame and the elder for peace and calm. The succession of the elder was secured by his great-aunt Maryam Begum and by the court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>. He was first enthroned on 7 August 1694, a week after his father's death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Soltan Hoseyn was nicknamed Darvish and Molla for his piety, and he left much of the government to ministers, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> grand vizier Fath-Ali Khan Daghestani, in office from 1715. Clerics were allowed to pursue anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> measures and action against non-Shi'ites, including the forced conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a> and the collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Christians, yet he also issued decrees protecting Christians and allowing missionaries to work. The anti-Sunni policies alienated many Sunnis, most of whom lived on the frontiers.</p><p>The reign saw growing disorder. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochi-language">Balochi</a> invasion overwhelmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> in 1699, and in 1703 the Georgian prince Gorgin Khan was made governor of Qandahar, where he came into conflict with the Afghan chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirwais-hotak">Mirwais Hotak</a>. In 1721 an earthquake destroyed three quarters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and killed over 40,000 people. The weight of the abbasi coin was cut from 7.39 g to 5.34 g between 1717 and 1722.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mirwais's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-hotak">Mahmud Hotak</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 1722, and famine forced Soltan Hoseyn to surrender on 21 October 1722. His third son declared himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-ii">Tahmasp II</a> at Qazvin in November. In February 1725 Mahmud ordered the other princes killed; Soltan Hoseyn was wounded trying to stop it, but saved two young children. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashraf-hotak">Ashraf Hotak</a> had Soltan Hoseyn beheaded on 9 September 1727, answering an insulting message from the Ottoman commander Ahmad Pasha. Contemporary and later writers have counted his devotion, superstition, pursuit of pleasure and extravagance among the causes of the decline of the Safavid state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Soltan Hoseyn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After surrendering Isfahan in 1722, <b>Shah Sultan Husayn</b> personally placed the crown on the head of the Afghan conqueror Mir Mahmud Hotak.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Mahmud Hotak invaded Persia, defeated the Safavid army at Gulnabad in 1722 and took Isfahan after a long siege, forcing <b>Shah Sultan Husayn</b> to abdicate.</blockquote><a href="/state/hotak-dynasty">Hotak dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1708" aria-label="Isfahan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5876 977 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sultan Husayn"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6326 1247h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6326 1247h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1708">What was here in 1708? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
